| Permission     | Meaning                                                                   | Common use          |
| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------- |
| **SUID**       | File runs with the **owner's permission**                                 | Executable programs |
| **SGID**       | File runs with the **group's permission** / files inherit directory group | Shared directories  |
| **Sticky Bit** | In a directory, users can delete **only their own files**                 | `/tmp`              |

1. SUID — Set User ID
Concept

Normally, when you run a file, it runs with your user's permissions.

With SUID, an executable runs with the owner's permissions.

For example:

File owner = root
SUID set
        ↓
Normal user runs the program
        ↓
Program temporarily runs with root's permissions

=> How to identify SUID

Use:

ls -l /usr/bin/passwd

You may see:

-rwsr-xr-x 1 root root ... /usr/bin/passwd

Notice:

rws
  ^
  s = SUID

Normally execute permission is:

x

With SUID it becomes:

s

=> Lab 1: Set SUID
Step 1 — Create a test script
mkdir ~/special-permissions-lab
cd ~/special-permissions-lab

Create:

nano test.sh

Put:

#!/bin/bash
id

Save and exit.

Make it executable:

chmod +x test.sh

Run:

./test.sh

You should see your normal user:

uid=1000(...)

Step 2 — Change owner to root
sudo chown root:root test.sh

Check:

ls -l test.sh

Step 3 — Set SUID
sudo chmod u+s test.sh

Check:

ls -l test.sh

You may see:

-rwsr-xr-x 1 root root ... test.sh

The important part:

rws
  ^
 SUID

Important:-

SUID is mainly meaningful on executable binaries.

Check:

ls -l /usr/bin/passwd

You will typically see:

-rwsr-xr-x root root ...

Why does passwd need SUID?

A normal user needs to change their password, but password-related files are protected and owned by root.

So:

Normal user
     ↓
runs passwd
     ↓
passwd has SUID
     ↓
runs with root's effective privileges

2. SGID — Set Group ID

SGID has two common meanings.

On a file

An executable with SGID runs with the file's group permissions.

On a directory

This is very useful for Linux administration.

When SGID is set on a directory:

New files/directories created inside inherit the directory's group.

This is commonly used for shared project directories.

=> Lab 2: SGID on Directory

Let's create two users.

sudo useradd user1
sudo useradd user2

Create a group:

sudo groupadd project

Add both users:

sudo usermod -aG project user1
sudo usermod -aG project user2

Check:

getent group project

You should see something similar to:

project:x:1001:user1,user2

=> Step 1 — Create shared directory
sudo mkdir /shared-project

Change group:

sudo chown root:project /shared-project

Set permissions:

sudo chmod 2775 /shared-project

Check:

ls -ld /shared-project

You should see something similar to:

drwxrwsr-x root project /shared-project

Look at:

drwxrwsr-x
     ^
     s

That s means SGID.

=> Step 2 — Create a file

Switch to user1:

sudo -iu user1

--------------
-i = login shell
It switches to user1 as if user1 has logged in normally. It loads user1's environment, home directory, etc.
-u = user
Specifies which user you want to switch to.
--------------

Check group:

id

Create a file:

cd /shared-project
touch file1

Check:

ls -l file1

You should see:

-rw-r--r-- user1 project file1

Notice:

owner = user1
group = project

The file inherited the directory's group.

That's the important purpose of SGID on directories.

Exit user1:

exit

3. Sticky Bit

Sticky Bit is mainly used on directories.

Concept

Suppose you have:

/shared

and many users can create files there.

a) Without Sticky Bit:

user1 creates file1
user2 may be able to delete file1

Even though file1 belongs to user1, user2 may be able to delete it if the directory permissions allow deletion.

b) With Sticky Bit
user1 → creates file1
user2 → tries to delete file1
        ↓
       ❌ Permission denied

user2 cannot delete user1's file.

But these users can delete it:

user1       → ✅ file owner
/shared owner → ✅ directory owner
root         → ✅ root

The classic example is:

/tmp

Check it:

ls -ld /tmp

You will usually see something like:

drwxrwxrwt root root /tmp

You will usually see something like:

drwxrwxrwt root root /tmp

The final:

t

means Sticky Bit.

