This is interpreted incorrectly, so sudo thinks i is the username:

sudo: user 'i' not found

-------------

Exit:

exit

Step 2 — Try deleting as user2
sudo -iu user2

Go to the directory:

cd /home/YOUR_USERNAME/sticky-lab

Try:

rm user1-file

Because the directory is 777 and there is no Sticky Bit, user2 may be able to delete it

Exit:

exit

Step 3 — Enable Sticky Bit
chmod +t ~/sticky-lab

Check:

ls -ld ~/sticky-lab

Now:

drwxrwxrwt
         ^
         t

The t means Sticky Bit.

Step 4 — Test again

Create another file as user1:

sudo -iu user1

cd /home/YOUR_USERNAME/sticky-lab
touch user1-file2
exit

Now become user2:

sudo -iu user2

Try:

cd /home/YOUR_USERNAME/sticky-lab
rm user1-file2

You should get:

Operation not permitted

That's Sticky Bit.

